    historyContent: {
      origins: "Klondike itself originated during the 19th-century gold rush in the Yukon. The 'Double' version emerged in the early 20th century as players sought a more complex and longer-lasting challenge than the standard 52-card version.",
      originCountries: [
        'Canada',
        'UK',
      ],
      timeline: [
        'Late 1800s: Standard Klondike becomes popular in mining camps.',
        '1920s: Double-deck solitaire variations begin appearing in rulebooks like Hoyle.',
        '1990s: Becomes a staple in digital solitaire collections on personal computers.',
      ],
      evolution: "The expansion from 7 to 9 tableau columns was designed to maintain the balance of hidden cards while providing enough 'legal move' space for the 104-card deck.",
      cultural: "Often played as a relaxing yet mentally stimulating solo activity; recognized as one of the most popular 'big' solitaire games.",
